#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:7
msgid "Source Control Best Practices"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:11
msgid "Table of Contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:14
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:15
msgid ""
"This page highlights source control considerations commonly encountered when "
"contributing to ROS 2 projects. It is not intended to replace Git "
"documentation. Instead, it focuses on ROS-specific recommendations and "
"references to external resources where appropriate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:20
msgid "Avoid committing generated and temporary files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:22
msgid ""
"ROS 2 workspaces generate build artifacts that should generally not be "
"committed to source control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:25
msgid "Sources of generated, temporary, and backup files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:28
msgid "Colcon workspace artifacts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:29
msgid "Common workspace artifacts include:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:31
msgid ""
"build/\n"
"install/\n"
"log/"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:37
msgid ""
"The ``build/``, ``install/``, and ``log/`` directories are generated by "
"``colcon build`` as part of a ROS 2 workspace. These directories are "
"typically created at the workspace level rather than inside individual "
"repositories."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:40
msgid ""
"If these directories appear within a repository (e.g. by accidentally "
"calling ``colcon build`` in a wrong directory), they should not be committed "
"to source control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:43
msgid "Python-generated files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:45
msgid ""
"Python generates several temporary files, including the ``__pycache__/`` "
"directory and files ending with ``*.pyc``, ``*.pyo``, and ``*.pyd`` when you "
"run the program. None of these files should ever be committed to a "
"repository."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:49
msgid "IDE configuration files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:51
msgid ""
"Editors and IDEs often generate project-specific or user-specific "
"configuration files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:53
msgid "Common examples include:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:55
msgid ""
".vscode/\n"
".idea/"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:60
msgid ""
"Many ROS 2 repositories ignore editor-specific files such as ``.vscode/`` "
"and ``.idea/``. Contributors should avoid committing personal editor "
"configuration unless it is explicitly required by the repository."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:64
msgid "Editor temporary and backup files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:66
msgid ""
"Some editors like Vim create backup files ending with ``~`` (for example, "
"``test.py~``) and temporary files ending with ``*.swp``, ``*.swo``, and "
"similar. These should never be committed to the repository."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:70
msgid "Operating system files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:72
msgid ""
"Operating systems may generate metadata files such as ``.DS_Store`` (macOS) "
"and ``Thumbs.db`` (Windows). These files should never be committed to a "
"repository."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:75
msgid ""
"Before committing changes, review the files included in a commit. Ensure "
"that generated and temporary files have not been added accidentally."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:79
msgid "Gitignore placement options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:82
msgid "Repository ``.gitignore``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:84
msgid ""
"Use the following rule of thumb when deciding whether a file belongs in a "
"repository-specific ``.gitignore`` or your global ``.gitignore``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:86
msgid ""
"If the file will be present on every developer's computer, put it in the "
"repository ``.gitignore``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:89
msgid "Global ``.gitignore``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:91
msgid ""
"A global gitignore can help prevent accidentally committing files that are "
"unrelated to a repository, such as operating system metadata files or "
"personal editor configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:93
msgid ""
"Git supports configuring a global excludes file through the ``core."
"excludesfile`` configuration option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:95
msgid "For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:97
msgid "$ git config --global core.excludesfile ~/.gitignore_global"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:101
msgid ""
"A global gitignore is useful for excluding files that are specific to a "
"developer's machine or editor and are not intended to be committed to any "
"repository."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:104
msgid "Credential helpers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:106
msgid ""
"When contributing to ROS 2 repositories, contributors will often interact "
"with Git hosting services such as GitHub."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:108
msgid ""
"SSH keys and Git credential helpers can simplify authentication. Avoid "
"repeatedly entering credentials when pushing changes or interacting with "
"remote repositories."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:111
msgid ""
"Rather than duplicating setup instructions here, refer to the official "
"GitHub and Git documentation for recommended configuration steps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:114
msgid "Developing on shared robots"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:116
msgid ""
"When developing on a shared robot or other remote system, never copy your "
"private SSH keys there and never run a remote SSH agent or credential helper "
"that could cache your keys and make them available to others. Instead, use a "
"local SSH agent on your private machine and use ``ssh -A`` to temporarily "
"forward this agent to the remote end. Using a forwarded SSH agent makes your "
"SSH keys available on the remote machine and it also relieves you from "
"repeatedly entering your SSH key passphrase. If all developers log in as the "
"same user, there is a risk that someone else could reuse your forwarded "
"agent, so try to limit the amount of time spent in an ``ssh -A`` shell to "
"the very minimum. Refer to the official Git SSH documentation for setup "
"instructions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Source-Control-Best-Practices.rst:122
msgid ""
"If the remote machine is trusted, you can also set up a (read-only) deploy "
"key or deploy token so that it has access to your private repos. If you need "
"to push from this machine, use the SSH agent forwarding described above."
msgstr ""

